Title: Mary Had a Little Lamb
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/2/2026
Notes: Medium = stylized children's picture-book illustration; hand-painted pastoral colors, soft paper texture, and gentle storybook movement for all characters and animals.

FADE IN:

EXT. MARY'S COTTAGE YARD - MORNING

A painted country cottage stands beside a white fence. MARY, a young girl with brown braids, a blue pinafore, white apron, and straw bonnet, strokes a small lamb with snowy fleece and a red ribbon at its neck.

(SOUND: sparrows chirp, a wooden gate softly creaks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mary had a little lamb.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Its fleece was white as snow.

The lamb presses against Mary's blue skirt as she lifts her school satchel.

(MUSIC: a light flute melody follows Mary's footsteps)

EXT. COUNTRY LANE - MORNING

MARY walks along the winding lane, her straw bonnet bobbing. The little lamb trots faithfully behind her, its red ribbon bright against its white fleece.

(SOUND: small hooves patter on the dusty road)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And everywhere that Mary went, the lamb was sure to go.

MARY pauses beside a wildflower bank. The lamb stops when she stops, then follows when she continues.

(SOUND: a lamb bleats softly, leaves rustle in the breeze)

EXT. SCHOOLHOUSE YARD - MORNING

A one-room schoolhouse rises beyond a grassy yard. MARY approaches its door with her satchel. The lamb slips through the open gate behind her.

(SOUND: a brass school bell rings across the yard)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He followed her to school one day.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That was against the rule.

MARY looks back. The lamb gazes up at her and follows her through the schoolhouse door.

(MUSIC: the flute melody turns playfully mischievous)

INT. SCHOOLHOUSE - MORNING

Sunlight falls over wooden desks, slates, and a blackboard. TEACHER, a middle-aged woman in a dark gray dress with a white collar and pinned brown hair, faces her pupils. MARY sits quietly while the lamb stands beside her desk.

(SOUND: chalk scratches on the blackboard, children whisper)

ELI, a freckled boy in a brown waistcoat, points at the lamb. CLARA, a girl with dark curls and a yellow ribbon, covers a laugh.

ELI
Look! A lamb at school!

The lamb nuzzles Mary's hand. The children laugh, rise from their benches, and skip around their desks.

(SOUND: children laugh, chair legs scrape the floorboards)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It made the children laugh and play to see a lamb at school.

TEACHER steps toward the lamb and points firmly to the door.

TEACHER
Out, little lamb. School is no place for you.

MARY lowers her eyes. The lamb turns once toward her, then pads out through the open door.

(SOUND: the schoolhouse door shuts with a gentle thud)

EXT. SCHOOLHOUSE YARD - DAY

The lamb stands outside the closed schoolhouse door. Its snowy fleece catches the afternoon light; its red ribbon stirs in the wind.

(SOUND: wind moves through the grass, distant recitation muffled behind the door)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so the teacher turned him out.

The lamb circles the porch, then settles beneath the window where Mary's blue sleeve can be glimpsed inside.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But still he lingered near.

(SOUND: the lamb gives one patient bleat)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And waited patiently about till Mary did appear.

EXT. SCHOOLHOUSE YARD - AFTERNOON

The final school bell rings. MARY emerges in her blue pinafore and straw bonnet, carrying her satchel. The lamb leaps up from beneath the window.

(SOUND: the school bell rings, the lamb's hooves race over the grass)

The lamb runs to MARY and lays its head upon her arm. MARY wraps both arms around its neck.

(MUSIC: warm strings rise beneath the bell's fading echo)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then he ran to her, and laid his head upon her arm.

MARY rests her cheek against the lamb's white fleece.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
As if he said, “I'm not afraid.”

(SOUND: the lamb breathes softly against Mary's sleeve)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
“You'll keep me from all harm.”

EXT. SCHOOLHOUSE YARD - AFTERNOON

TEACHER stands on the porch as ELI and CLARA gather near MARY and the lamb. The children crouch carefully, offering open hands instead of grabbing at the animal.

(SOUND: bees hum around the clover, children murmur quietly)

CLARA
What makes the lamb love Mary so?

ELI
Why does he follow her?

TEACHER looks at MARY, whose hand remains gentle on the lamb's head.

TEACHER
Oh, Mary loves the lamb, you know.

(MUSIC: soft fiddle and flute begin)

TEACHER
And you each gentle animal in confidence may bind.

The children listen as the lamb calmly accepts CLARA'S careful touch.

TEACHER
And make them follow at your call, if you are always kind.

(SOUND: the lamb bleats once, answered by the children's quiet laughter)

EXT. COUNTRY LANE - SUNSET

MARY walks home along the country lane, her brown braids and blue pinafore glowing in the painted sunset. The little lamb with its red ribbon walks at her side, never behind.

(SOUND: evening crickets begin, soft hooves tap the lane)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And everywhere that Mary went, the lamb was sure to go.

MARY opens the cottage gate. The lamb follows her into the warm yard.

(MUSIC: the flute melody resolves gently)

FADE OUT.

THE END
